# SH-CoW Face-A NESTED shape: `grid.get(i).unwrap().get(j).unwrap().field = v`
# through a Vector-of-Vector. The self-host place arm matches the OUTER
# `.get(j).unwrap()` chain and lowers the INNER `grid.get(i).unwrap()` as the
# collection via `lower_place_base → lower_expr` — i.e. a VALUE COPY of the inner
# `Vector[Blk]` HANDLE. Because a Vector handle shares its heap buffer, the outer
# `gorget_array_get` still points INTO the real element buffer, so the write
# reaches the owner and this ALREADY write-throughs correctly on the self-host
# (measured SH = 99, ASan-clean) — the same incidental shared-buffer aliasing
# that makes the Face-B method-receiver path work.
#
# CORRECT output is the write-through 99 (Rust gg C + LLVM AND the self-host all
# agree). It is parked in `known_gaps/` NOT because it produces a wrong answer,
# but because the mechanism is INCIDENTAL (the inner chain is a value-copy that
# happens to share the buffer), not the principled write-through-Ptr place
# resolution — a robust deep-chain place producer (resolving the inner
# `.get().unwrap()` to a Ptr too, so a non-buffer-sharing inner element would
# also write through) is the deferred follow-on. Kept out of the runtime-diff
# corpus so the incidental pass is not mistaken for the principled guarantee.

struct Blk:
    int term

void main():
    Vector[Vector[Blk]] grid = [[Blk(10)]]
    grid.get(0).unwrap().get(0).unwrap().term = 99
    print(grid.get(0).unwrap().get(0).unwrap().term)   # 99 (write-through)
    print("done")
